From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 16:49:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen-mapcache: let QEMU_XEN_MAPCACHE_MAX_MB cap the foreign
 mapcache

On a 64-bit host the foreign mapcache is capped at 32 GB, i.e. one
bucket per 64 KiB of any guest, so buckets never collide. Together with
this layer's change that keeps unlocked foreign bucket heads mapped,
every guest page that has ever been DMAed through stays mapped.

On Arm each foreign mapping lives in the kernel's unpopulated "Xen
scratch" space, which is sized from the hypervisor's extended regions:
17 x 128 MB in the Raspberry Pi 4 DomD, for a 2 GB DomA plus blobs and
grants. Once the mapcache covers most of DomA, the next privcmd mapping
fails ("Cannot allocate new IOMEM resource", xenforeignmemory_map2:
ENOMEM) and the device model dies. This was seen during a 226 MB read
and a 286 MB adb install in DomA.

QEMU_XEN_MAPCACHE_MAX_MB, read from the device model's environment, sets
a smaller cap for the foreign (guest RAM) mapcache. With a cap, an
unlocked bucket head that collides is remapped instead of a new mapping
piling up. Values below 64 or not below the built-in cap are ignored;
unset (the default) keeps the built-in cap. The grant mapcache is not
affected: its entries are unmapped when their lock count drops to zero
anyway.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [this layer's Xen device model; switched by an environment variable]
---
 hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c b/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
index def688817..c617c92ef 100644
--- a/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
+++ b/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
@@ -184,9 +184,35 @@ void xen_map_cache_init(phys_offset_to_gaddr_t f, void *opaque)
         }
     }
 
-    mapcache = xen_map_cache_init_single(f, opaque,
-                                         bucket_shift,
-                                         max_mcache_size);
+    /*
+     * QEMU_XEN_MAPCACHE_MAX_MB caps the cache of FOREIGN mappings (guest RAM).
+     *
+     * WHY. A 64-bit host gets a 32 GB cap, i.e. one bucket per 64 KiB of any
+     * guest, so nothing ever collides, and with bucket heads kept mapped once
+     * unlocked (0224) every page the guest has ever DMAed through stays mapped.
+     * On Arm each foreign mapping lives in the kernel's unpopulated "Xen
+     * scratch" space, which is sized from the hypervisor's extended regions:
+     * 17 x 128 MB on RPi4 DomD, for a 2 GB DomA plus blobs and grants. Once the
+     * mapcache covers most of DomA, the next privcmd map fails ("Cannot allocate
+     * new IOMEM resource", xenforeignmemory_map2: ENOMEM) and the device model
+     * dies -- seen twice on 2026-10-03, during a 226 MB read and a 286 MB adb
+     * install. With a cap, an unlocked head that collides is remapped instead
+     * of a new one piling up. Grants are unaffected: their entries are unmapped
+     * at lock zero anyway.
+     */
+    {
+        const char *cap = getenv("QEMU_XEN_MAPCACHE_MAX_MB");
+        unsigned long mb = cap ? strtoul(cap, NULL, 10) : 0;
+
+        if (mb >= 64 && (mb << 20) < max_mcache_size) {
+            info_report("xen mapcache: foreign cache capped at %lu MB", mb);
+            mapcache = xen_map_cache_init_single(f, opaque, bucket_shift,
+                                                 mb << 20);
+        } else {
+            mapcache = xen_map_cache_init_single(f, opaque, bucket_shift,
+                                                 max_mcache_size);
+        }
+    }
 
     /*
      * Grant mappings must use XC_PAGE_SIZE granularity since we can't
